th sharp pain in the left lung"],"extremitiesAndJoints":["Pains in the limbs and joints as if sprained","Sensation of a string around the limb","Joints swollen and very sensitive","Trembling with a numb sensation"]},"fever":{"chill":["Chilliness with sensitivity to draughts"],"heat":["Heat with thirst and headache"],"sweat":["Profuse, debilitating sweat"],"pattern":"Intermittent and periodic","periodicity":"Classically returns every other day","time":["Paroxysms tend to recur at the same hour"],"stages":["Cold stage with chilliness","Hot stage with thirst","Sweating stage with profound debility"],"associatedSymptoms":["Thirst","Headache","Tympanitic distension"],"modalities":["Aggravated by draughts and touch"]},"haemorrhage":{"characterOfBlood":"Dark or bright according to the source, with marked debility afterwards","causes":["Loss of vital fluids","Haemorrhage from any orifice"],"associatedSymptoms":["Trembling","Ringing and buzzing in the ears","Anaemia and prostration"]},"discharges":{"character":"Exhausting discharges that leave the patient weak","modalities":["Weakness is proportional to the loss of fluid"]},"sensations":["Bursting sensation in the head","Brain balancing to and fro","String-like sensation around a limb","Bruised feeling in the head","Tympanitic fullness of the abdomen"],"locationAndDirection":{"location":["Head","Spleen and abdomen","Limbs and joints"]},"concomitants":["Flatulent dyspepsia with other complaints","Tinnitus with haemorrhage","Debility with every discharge"],"pediatric":{"general":["Debility after exhausting illness"],"behaviour":["Apathetic and indifferent"],"fever":["Intermittent fever in children"],"digestive":["Flatulent colic","Diarrhoea after eating"],"characteristicSymptoms":["Hydrocephaloid state after exhausting diarrhoea (traditional description)"]},"constitutionAndLifeStage":{"constitution":"Debilitated, sensitive and anaemic","age":"Any age; the classical picture follows depletion rather than age","lifeStage":"After haemorrhage, childbirth, exhausting illness or fluid loss","typicalPatientPicture":"A weakened, pale and oversensitive person who is worse from the slightest touch and whose complaints return at regular intervals."},"compareWith":[{"medicine":"Arsenicum album","similarities":["Debility","Gastrointestinal disturbance","Chilliness"],"differences":["Arsenicum has marked anxiety and restlessness; Cinchona is more apathetic and despondent","Arsenicum debility typically follows overexertion; Cinchona debility follows loss of vital fluids","Cinchona is far more strongly periodic"]},{"medicine":"Ferrum metallicum","similarities":["Anaemia","Weakness","Flushed face"],"differences":["Ferrum suits pure anaemia with a plethoric appearance","Cinchona is indicated when debility follows loss of fluids","Ferrum is listed as complementary to Cinchona"]},{"medicine":"Carbo vegetabilis","similarities":["Flatulence","Weakness","Diarrhoea"],"differences":["Carbo vegetabilis has coldness and collapse with desire for air","The Cinchona stool is induced by every attempt to eat or drink","Cinchona has marked periodicity, which Carbo vegetabilis lacks"]},{"medicine":"Phosphoric acid","similarities":["Debility with copious stools"],"differences":["Phosphoric acid stools are frequent and copious without proportionate debility","Cinchona stools are followed by marked prostration"]}],"relationships":{"complementary":["Ferrum metallicum","Calcarea phosphorica"],"antidotes":["Arnica montana","Arsenicum album","Nux vomica","Ipecacuanha"],"followsWell":["Calcarea phosphorica (in hydrocephaloid states)"],"relatedRemedies":["Digitalis purpurea (listed as incompatible after)","Selenium (listed as incompatible after)"]},"therapeuticApplications":{"note":"The following are traditional homeopathic Materia Medica associations for educational study only. They are not evidence-based medical treatment recommendations. Intermittent fevers such as malaria require proper medical diagnosis and treatment.","applications":["Traditional association with periodic and intermittent fevers","Debility following haemorrhage or other loss of vital fluids","Flatulent dyspepsia with tympanitic distension","Haemorrhages accompanied by weakness and tinnitus","Pains in the limbs and joints with marked touch sensitivity"]},"quickRevision":{"keyPoints":["Loss of vital fluids","Periodicity every other day","Oversensitive to touch","Tympanitic abdomen","Debility with haemorrhage"],"mind":["Apathetic","Despondent","Crowding of ideas"],"physicalGenerals":["Chilly and sensitive to draughts","Profound debility","Anaemia"],"modalities":["Aggravation from slightest touch and after eating","Amelioration from hard pressure and bending double"],"foodAndThirst":["Thirst during fever","Eating aggravates"],"majorSystems":["Digestive","Spleen and liver","Blood","Nervous"],"characteristicSymptoms":["Debility from loss of vital fluids","Periodicity every other day","Slightest touch aggravates, hard pressure relieves"]},"examFocus":{"importantConcepts":["Periodicity","Debility from loss of fluids","Hypersensitivity to touch","Tympanites","Intermittent fever"],"frequentlyAskedTopics":["Characteristic symptoms of Cinchona officinalis","Cinchona in intermittent fever","Comparison of Cinchona with Arsenicum album and Ferrum metallicum"],"shortNoteTopics":["Cinchona officinalis","China in debility from loss of vital fluids"],"compareQuestions":["Cinchona versus Arsenicum album in debility","Cinchona versus Ferrum metallicum in anaemia","Cinchona versus Carbo vegetabilis in flatulent dyspepsia"],"feverTopics":["Periodicity in intermittent fever","Thirst and sweat in the Cinchona fever picture"],"characteristicSymptoms":["Every other day periodicity","Slightest touch aggravates","Tympanitic distension"],"modalities":["Aggravation from touch, draughts and after eating","Amelioration from hard pressure and bending double"]},"traditionalNote":"This entry organizes traditional Homeopathic Materia Medica descriptions for educational and examination-reference purposes. Cinchona officinalis is the source of quinine, a conventional antimalarial medicine; this profile does not suggest that homeopathic Cinchona is an evidence-based treatment for malaria or any other condition."}$materia_medica_json$::jsonb,
